---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Crumbs from '../components/Crumbs.astro';
import { atlas } from '../lib/atlas.ts';

const a = atlas();
---

<Base title="About" description="What the Skill Atlas is, how resources are chosen and checked, and what comes next.">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <Crumbs items={[{ label: 'About' }]} />
    <h1>About the atlas</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      ProductEngineer.pro is a general reference for product engineering, laid out as a map. Every territory, capability and skill
      has a short description, a few resources worth the time, and the tools people use for it.
    </p>

    <h2>Three levels</h2>
    <p>
      The atlas has {a.territories.length - 1} territories, plus a group of cross-cutting capabilities drawn as the waters around the
      land. Under them sit {a.capabilities.length} capabilities and {a.skills.length} skills. Every skill has one home on the map;
      when it matters elsewhere, other pages link to it instead of repeating it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The taxonomy is a proposed framework, not an industry certification. Not every skill needs the same depth: a product engineer
      should do the core work independently, make informed decisions in the wider areas, and go deep selectively.
    </p>

    <h2>How resources are chosen</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Written or recorded by practitioners or recognised sources, and the primary source over a summary of it.</li>
      <li>Free to read or watch. A paid book or course appears only when it is the standard reference, and is marked as paid.</li>
      <li>Still accurate, in English, and dated.</li>
      <li>No link is written from memory. Each one is opened and checked before it is saved, and a link checker re-runs on a schedule.</li>
      <li>Tools: widely used ones, with an open-source or free option where one exists. No affiliate links.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Two good links beat a full quota, so some topics have fewer.</p>

    <h2>Your progress</h2>
    <p>
      You can mark each skill as learning or comfortable. That status is stored only in your browser. There is no account and nothing
      is sent to a server. Export a file from <a href="/me/">your map</a> to keep a copy.
    </p>

    <h2>Lit maps</h2>
    <p>
      A link can light any set of nodes and join them with a route, for example
      <a href="/atlas/?lit=user-interviews,hypotheses,sample-size,funnel-analysis">/atlas/?lit=user-interviews,hypotheses,sample-size,funnel-analysis</a>.
      It is a way to show which parts of the discipline a piece of work exercised. Later versions will light the map from experiments
      run on real products.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>Page analytics, when enabled, are cookieless and collect no personal data. Videos load from YouTube only when you press play.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
